a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/6c/6cf95f58e436577be5ad696325ae2d1e7bfd5882b4899e88806c4ae653a5c518.png)\n\n  \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:  \n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,6]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi /2]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 6,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi /2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281349 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281067 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281349 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161833" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281349 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281349 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b2/b221cb7e202e72f500416481175dd55b87c6a129dbdc121e20bcfdb7f8d0d443.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the quarter-sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4$ for $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\le 0,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281349 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2cf460d5-5691-56a1-870f-c5b2d51af522" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281349 #edb/ref 1074 "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/cc/cca15d8d91ebfe643777122cf41a49f2bfe3bac901b30e160cad65eb6b4b102f.png)\n\n  \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary withing the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,2]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281370 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281370 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161883" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281370 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281370 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a3/a32e8fd24c990a831cfee9525c6b1c035fb4ee6e09deedb3ac4c899d90ceeaac.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the half-sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4y=0$ for $z\\le 0,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281370 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5c94b1ab-44af-5e40-9fea-2f1ea696e7ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281370 #edb/ref 1074 "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-half of the sphere of radius $2$ centered at $(0,-2,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4y=0$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4y & =0 \\\\ {\\rho }^{2}+4\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ & =0 \\\\ \\rho (\\rho +4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/e2/e2865e838521a8864ad76d72e520d4b128a8fceb8c18cb9c35ba10134419c4eb.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363431 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363431 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363431 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363431 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363431 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363431 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363431 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363431 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186363432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363431 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "acc82db6-7cde-56cc-80d8-b767a5862ad1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363432 #edb/ref 1080 "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363432 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363432 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "74af2fbc-b192-5afc-8573-9975539886f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363433 #edb/ref 1080 "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }" #edb/re